//! The jit capability (Phase 18, ADR 0624): what lets a launcher give a
//! program memory.jit, so that the program may make its private anonymous
//! memory readable, writable and executable at once, as a JavaScript
//! engine or another compiler that makes machine code as it runs does.
//! Without it, no page is ever writable and executable at once
//! (radix-address-space(2)'s `map_anonymous` and `protect` fail with
//! invalid_argument, and a Linux program's `mmap` and `mprotect` with
//! EACCES). A file's pages and shared memory never are, memory.jit or
//! not.
//!
//! A process given memory.jit keeps it until it ends: `fork` copies it to
//! the child and `exec` keeps it; a process `create` makes starts without
//! it. Holding the capability gives a process nothing by itself: its
//! holder gives memory.jit to a process it starts, before that process
//! runs its program. `/processes/<n>/status` says whether a process has
//! it (radix-proc(5)).
//!
//! The kernel gives Spark the one capability, with the control right.
//! Spark gives authd a copy when its service file asks for memory.jit,
//! and authd shares one into SLOT_JIT of each session it signs a person
//! in to, so that `bundle run` gives memory.jit to a bundle's program
//! whose grants hold it and `linux run --allow-jit` to a Linux program.

module jit;

/// The authority to give processes memory.jit. Every method needs the
/// control right.
interface jit = 0x0036 version 1 {
    kind = jit;
    /// Gives the process in slot `process` memory.jit, which it keeps
    /// until it ends. Needs the control right on the process too.
    /// invalid_argument if it is the caller's own process: a holder gives
    /// memory.jit, it does not take it.
    method allow = 1 (process: slot) -> ()
        raises [no_such_slot, empty_slot, wrong_kind, missing_rights, invalid_argument];
    /// Whether the process in slot `process` has memory.jit (`given`), and
    /// whether it asked for memory that is writable and executable
    /// without it and was refused (`refused`), as `linux run` asks once
    /// its program has ended, to say that `--allow-jit` would have let it.
    /// Needs the read right on the process too, and works on a process
    /// that has ended while the capability lasts.
    method state = 2 (process: slot) -> (given: bool, refused: bool)
        raises [no_such_slot, empty_slot, wrong_kind, missing_rights];
}
